<GuidePage title="Designing for the Unknown - App Router Guide" root="..">
<div class="kicker">Chapter 11 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
<h1>Designing for the Unknown</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Everything in <a href="09-containers-content-shifting.html">§9</a> and
  <a href="10-suspense-streaming.html">§10</a> assumed the layout <em>can</em> be
  contained. That is not an engineering given; it is decided earlier, in design. Some
  layouts are containable and some fight containment no matter how carefully we implement
  them, which makes stability a cross-team goal: engineering, UX, and design deciding
  together what the page does while the data is still unknown.
</p>

<h2 id="shared-goal">11.1 Stability is a shared goal, not an engineering metric</h2>
<p>
  When we bring this problem to design as "we need to reduce CLS," we have already lost
  the conversation. Cumulative Layout Shift is an engineering measurement, not a shared
  vocabulary: nobody outside the codebase should need to know what the acronym means, and
  leading with it turns a design conversation into a compliance request. The goal
  underneath the metric is one designers care about more than we do:
  <strong>the page must not move under the user.</strong> Nothing they are reading jumps,
  nothing they are about to click dodges, nothing "settles" in three visible steps. Say it
  that way and design is on our side immediately; say "CLS" and we are negotiating against
  a number.
</p>
<p>
  The reason the conversation has to happen at all is timing.
  <Chip k="very" /> By the time engineering discovers a
  layout is unstable, the design is approved, the component is half built, and every fix
  is a compromise: a hardcoded height that is wrong half the time, a spinner where a
  skeleton should be, a container that scrolls awkwardly because the space it was given
  never matched its content. The cheap moment to fix instability is
  <em>before the mock is approved</em>, and engineering is the only party in the room who
  knows what the page looks like during the states the mock does not show. That makes it
  our job to bring the question, not design's job to anticipate it.
</p>

<Callout kind="voice" title="The translation">
  <p>
    Never "this design has CLS problems." Instead: "walk me through what this page shows
    while the tables are still loading" and "what should happen when this list has 50
    rows and that one has 2?" The first framing asks design to fix an engineering number;
    the second hands them a design problem, which is what it actually is.
  </p>
</Callout>

<h2 id="happy-path">11.2 The comp is a happy path</h2>
<p>
  A Figma comp is a snapshot of exactly one state: the <strong>happy path</strong>. The
  data has arrived, every region is populated, and the row counts were chosen so the
  composition looks balanced inside the frame: the table shows the six rows that fit, the
  cards have names of pleasant length, and nothing is empty, loading, or overflowing.
  None of this is laziness; it is what comps are <em>for</em>: selling a composition. But
  a static tool produces static answers, and the states that break layouts are precisely
  the ones a static frame never has to confront: the second before the data arrives, the
  day the table has one row, the customer whose program list needs three screens of
  scrolling.
</p>
<p>
  So the questions engineering must put on the table, for every data-driven region in
  every mock, are these:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>"What does this region show before we know what's in it?"</strong> Not "a
    spinner": what shape, what height, what happens to its neighbors while it waits
    (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html#fallback-trade">§10.2</a>).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>"What happens when the content is much smaller than this?"</strong> One row.
    Zero rows: the empty state is a designed state, not a gap
    (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal">§10.6</a>).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>"What happens when the content is much larger than this?"</strong> Does the
    region grow (pushing what's below it), scroll, paginate, or truncate? Someone must
    pick one, and the pick changes the layout.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>"Which of these regions arrive at different times?"</strong> Two regions that
    stream independently (<a href="10-suspense-streaming.html">§10</a>) will resolve in
    either order; the design has to look intentional in both.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  A designer who has been asked these questions three times starts asking them without us.
  That is the actual deliverable of this chapter: not a fixed mock, but a design partner
  who thinks in states.
</p>

<h2 id="stacked-tables">11.3 The worked example: two stacked tables</h2>
<p>
  Here is the canonical hard case, and it looks completely innocent in a comp: two tables
  stacked vertically inside the app chrome, say <em>Assignments</em> above
  <em>Submissions</em> on a program page. In the mock, each shows a handful of rows and
  together they fill the content area exactly. Now walk the states. On first paint the
  row counts are unknown (and even with SSR, the <em>server</em> knows them but the
  <em>designer</em> still didn't when the heights were drawn). So how much vertical space
  does each table get?
</p>
<p>
  Every naive answer fails a different way. <strong>Split the height 50/50:</strong> the
  guess is wrong almost every time, because real data is skewed: the top table has 1
  assignment and sits in a sea of dead space while the bottom one crams 50 submissions
  into the same half and scrolls furiously. <strong>Size each table to its content with a
  min-height:</strong> now the pre-data state reserves the min-height, but the moment the
  top table's 30 rows arrive it grows and <em>shoves the bottom table down the page</em>,
  possibly below the fold: the exact reading-interrupting shift we exist to prevent, and
  it happens on every load, in front of the user.
</p>

